From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:29:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: edit and delete Signal messages, sent and received, in
 place

* Edit and delete the user's own Signal messages

The Messaging app can now edit or delete a message the user sent. The
transport asks for either with the "webos-im-send-edit" (account, chat,
serviceMessageId, text) and "webos-im-send-delete" (account, chat,
serviceMessageId) signals, which return TRUE from the plug-in that owns
the account and took the command. Both are connected once at login, as
the reaction signal is, and answer FALSE for an account of another
plug-in.

The serviceMessageId this plug-in hands out for a message is its sent
timestamp in milliseconds (the "webos-im-outbox-id" of an app-composed
message), which is how Signal names the message to edit or delete.

An edit goes out as a Signal EditMessage: the original's sent timestamp
and a new DataMessage with the new text, converted as send_im converts
a message. When the original is in the store its attachments, quote
and disappearing-message timer are carried over, so that only the text
changes. A delete is a DataMessage whose delete field names the sent
timestamp. For a group both carry the group context, as a group
message does. A phone number as the chat is resolved to the contact
first, as for a send.

Neither is shown in the conversation by the plug-in; the result is in
the debug log. Signal clients apply an edit or a delete only within
their own time limits, which this plug-in does not check.

* Change a Signal message where it is when it is edited or deleted

"Delete for everyone" was dropped, so the message stayed; an edit came
as a new message holding the new text, under the old one.

Both now change the message the Messaging transport keeps, by its
serviceMessageId (the sent timestamp): a deletion emits
"webos-im-delete" (account, id) and an edit "webos-im-edit" (account,
id, text), the text as a received message's body is, without the
quote of a reply, which the stored message does not hold either. The
same goes for our own messages edited or deleted on another device.
An edit fetches no attachments again: Signal edits only the text.

changes.rs holds the id and the text, with unit tests
(rustc --test changes.rs).

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
